-- title:
--   AP hypergraphs, degenerate simplices, and the uniform removal predicate
-- statement:
--   A finite cyclic set $A$ defines an unweighted AP-simplex hypergraph by requiring each AP edge-form value to lie in $A$. A constant progression is represented by a point of $A$ and a free tail in the AP/simplex coordinate equivalence. Given a simplex cover, a witnessing deleted colour can be chosen for each such degenerate simplex and encoded in a fixed finite set of coloured deletion slots.
--
--   The uniform cyclic partite-removal predicate says that, for every $\varepsilon>0$, there is a $c>0$ independent of the positive modulus such that any corresponding hypergraph with normalized simplex count below $c$ has a simplex cover of normalized deletion cost at most $\varepsilon$. The predicate records the exact combinatorial input used by the AP counting argument.
